    {
      "log_path": "stages/optimize/work/.kernelgen/claude-runtime.log",
      "log_sha256": "922c37efd8ebc4e3c9af5fe3613be96da91ea9dd8eb8d4f34b81419513f58bf1",
      "character_offset": 113919,
      "tool_use_id": "call_00_siI6uNvFfuXgANjZPPlG9919",
      "arguments": {
        "description": "Profile round 1 candidate",
        "subagent_type": "kernel-profile-analyzer",
        "prompt": "Profile measured KernelGen round_num=1 for operator special_round_out (a pure pointwise round(x, decimals=0/1) with out= semantics, float16/bfloat16/float32).\n\nMeasured facts from the authoritative eval (round 1, PASSED 30/30, geo_mean 0.2305, min_speedup 0.170 on timing-7):\n- candidate latency ~0.0627–0.0700 ms for the nine small timing workloads whose reference latency is only ~0.0142–0.0158 ms (e.g. timing-0 = 1024 fp16 elements: cand 0.0628 ms vs ref 0.0142 ms)\n- timing-3 (4096x4096 fp16): cand 0.1730 ms vs ref 0.0319 ms\n- timing-7: cand 0.1728 ms vs ref 0.0294 ms\n- timing-11 (4096x4096 fp32): cand 0.1804 ms vs ref 0.0789 ms\nThe flat small cases all cluster at a ~63 µs floor regardless of element count, which suggests host/launch overhead dominance, but I need device-side evidence to separate (a) host launch/METADATA overhead from (b) device kernel duration and (c) whether the device kernel is vector-instruction bound versus MTE2/MTE3 bandwidth bound.\n\nPlease collect backend-native (msprof) evidence for the round-1 snapshot and answer:\n1. Device kernel duration for a small case (timing-0 or timing-8) and for a large case (timing-3 or timing-11), plus the reference torch.round device kernel duration on the same case if the tooling allows.\n2. MTE2 / MTE3 / VEC (or equivalent) pipeline activity ratios, and any available instruction-count / scalar-vs-vector ratio, for those kernels. I want to know whether the device kernel is instruction bound (the current per-element math is ~12 fp32 vector ops: fp32 convert, multiply, abs, bitcast+and, add, sub, compare, select, bitcast+and+or, bitcast, optional reciprocal multiply, convert back) or memory bound.\n3. Whether the device time for the 4096x4096 fp16 case is consistent with 67 MB of traffic at achievable HBM/L2 bandwidth, or much slower than that.\n4. Whether masked (predicated) global loads/stores are visible in the generated code/instruction mix, since every program currently uses an `index < N` mask.\n\nThen persist a validated analysis via record_profile_analysis and return ONE actionable next experiment for the Coder, ranked by expected geo_mean gain across 9 small + 3 large timing workloads. Do not modify tmp/main.py."
      }
    }
  ],
